{
  "case_id": "2020-19-963",
  "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0000-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES: KANNON K. SHANMUGAM, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioner. DANIEL L. GEYSER, ESQUIRE, Dallas, Texas; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Henry Schein, Inc.",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case presents an important question concerning the interpretation of agreements that delegate questions of arbitrability to the arbitrator."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could you just give me -- help me with the delegation language in this contract, this arbitration agreement. Where is it? I don't see the word \"delegation\" at all or a verb \"delegate\" at all."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Henry Schein, Inc.",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The question is whether the incorporation of arbitration rules that authorize the arbitrator to resolve questions of arbitrability constitutes a valid delegation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What do you understand to be the basis for the presumption of arbitrability? It was recognized, I think, the first time in Moses Cohen, and the Court said that it was based on the federal policy favoring arbitration, and it cited Section 2 of the Federal Arbitration Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First Options makes it very clear that we have to have a clear delegation. But I think what the court below said was what is unclear is whether the form of the delegation here was intended to be included in that arbitrability section."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Good morning, Mr. Shanmugam. I want to make sure I have the roadmap clear here with you. The premise of the case as you're presenting it to us is that this contract language expressly delegates the question of arbitrability to the arbitrator, correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If you take a look at page 119 of the Joint Appendix, you'll see the AAA's model arbitration clause, and that provision is very similar to the provision at issue here except that it lacks the \"except for\" language. And as I indicated, you would have exactly the same question when you have a dispute about whether a claim is, in fact, arising under the contract or out of the contract."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That would be subject to very limited judicial review under the Federal Arbitration Act and under Section 10, but, of course, that's just by virtue of the operation of the Arbitration Act that that review is so limited."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: So, if I understand you then, you -- your argument is that -- well, who determines that? Would that then be the court?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Geyser",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GEYSER: Well, Your Honor, unless there's clear and unmistakable evidence that the parties wanted the arbitrator to decide arbitrability, then the default is with the court, and the court has to first identify a delegation agreement and identify any limits to that delegation agreement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: So it's not your position that it's possible to incorporate the AAA rules by reference but still, through the context of the language in the provision, not intend simply by reference --by incorporating the AAA rules to clearly and unmistakably delegate?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-19-963",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ba00b55ef43facb45889126c860e4c6a7a2f7afa30cad2997fa194f3a167be6a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba00b55ef43f-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: You have a minute to wrap up, counsel."
    },
    {
